README.vms -- VMS-specific notes on building and running FreeTDS

FreeTDS has successfully built and passed all expected tests on all supported
versions of OpenVMS, which as of this writing are OpenVMS Alpha (8.4+), OpenVMS
I64 (8.4+), and OpenVMS x86 (9.2+). In not too distant memory it has built and
run on most versions of OpenVMS 7.0 and later with DEC/Compaq C 6.0 or later.

Other prerequisites:

    gunzip
    vmstar
    MMK
    Perl (only to build from a git checkout)

Build Instructions

Decompress and unpack the source archive onto an ODS-5 disk using gunzip and
vmstar or other tar utility. Make sure extended parse is enabled in your
process (SET PROCESS/PARSE=EXTENDED) and that the feature logical name
DECC$EFS_CHARSET is enabled; these settings (and the ODS-5 disk) are not
required to run FreeTDS, but they are necessary for the build and test suite to
find their requisite parts.

Set default to the top-level source directory and
run the configuration script like so:

    $ @[.vms]configure

or if you are building from a github checkout (as opposed to a release tarball)

    $ @[.vms]configure_trunk

configure_trunk uses Perl to generate the source files that are included in
release tarballs.  Two headers, [.include.freetds]encodings.h and
[.include.freetds]charset_lookup.h, require gperf, which is not available on
VMS; copy them from a release tarball or from a build on another system
before running configure_trunk.

To build without thread safety pass --disable-thread-safe to the configuration
script:

    $ @[.vms]configure --disable-thread-safe

The configuration script creates config.mms, holding the detected
configuration, user.mms, holding your build options (it is created only if it
does not exist, and is never overwritten), and a descrip.mms in the top-level
source directory which you may execute by simply running MMK (a freeware MMS
alternative available from https://github.com/endlesssoftware/mmk):

    $ MMK

The build creates stage.com; run it to define foreign commands for the
utilities (freebcp, tsql, bsqldb, defncopy, tdspool and fisql):

    $ @stage.com

To clean the build use

    $ MMK clean

or "MMK distclean" to remove the configuration files too (including user.mms).
After building from a git checkout, "MMK maintainer-clean" also removes the
files generated by configure_trunk.

Build options

You may change some default behaviors by adding one or more of the following
macros to user.mms, one per line, for instance

    ODBC = 1
    ODBC_MARS = 1

ODBC

By default the build does not include ODBC support; this macro instructs the
build to include it.  At the moment, the unixODBC driver manager is assumed
(see https://www.unixodbc.org), but others may work as well.

The following logical names must be defined in order to build with ODBC
support:

    ODBC_LIBDIR		directory containing driver manager's libraries
    ODBC_INCDIR		directory containing driver manager's include files
    LIBODBC		the shareable image comprising the driver manager
    LIBODBCINST		the shareable image of the driver manager installer
			library

Note:  in unixODBC 2.2.4 and later, all of these logicals except
ODBC_INCDIR will be set up by running ODBC_SETUP.COM in the library
directory.

ODBC_MARS

Enables MARS (Multiple Active Result Sets) support in the ODBC driver.

ODBC_WIDE

Builds the ODBC UTF-16 (wide) API as well as the UTF-8 one.

ODBC_WIDETEST

Builds the ODBC tests using the UTF-16 API instead of the UTF-8 one.

MSDBLIB

Enables Microsoft behavior in the db-lib API where it diverges from
Sybase's.  See additional documentation in the [.doc] directory.

OPENSSL_STATIC

Links statically against OpenSSL.  OpenSSL is detected by the configuration
script if the logical name OPENSSL is defined.

__DEBUG__

Instructs the build to target the OpenVMS debugger and produce compiler listings
and linker maps.

FreeTDS negotiates with the server to find a reasonable version of the TDS
protocol.  It does not accept a compile-time override of this; you can override
it at runtime by specifying a version in the freetds.conf, or defining the
environment variable TDSVER.

Running the tests

Copy PWD.in to PWD in the top-level source directory and edit it to specify
the server, user name, password and database to use for the tests; point the
logical name FREETDSCONF to the freetds.conf file to use, then run

    $ MMK check

To run again only the tests that failed use

    $ MMK recheck

When testing ODBC configure odbc.ini and odbcinst.ini as required by your
driver manager, and make sure they are world-readable, since the tests run
under minimal privileges.

Caveats and To-do:

It's probably best to define the locations of your configuration files as
logical names; otherwise FreeTDS may concoct an unparseable combination of
UNIX and VMS filename syntax.  For example:

    $ DEFINE FREETDSCONF DISK$USER:[TDSDIR]freetds.conf

There is currently no install target; it's up to you to put the libraries,
include files, etc. wherever you want them.

A VAX port is within the realm of possibilities.  It would need to convert
between IEEE floating point data and native format and/or request native
F_FLOAT and D_FLOAT from the server (which may only work on Sybase, not MS
SQL Server, but that's TBD).

As more ODBC functions are implemented, the file [.vms]odbc_driver_axp.opt
needs to be updated to point to the real functions instead of the aliases to
the stub for unimplemented functions.

The build currently compiles with the /NAMES=AS_IS switch when the ODBC
macro is enabled; this results in mixed-case symbol names.  Mixed-case
symbols were necessary to get the code working with unixODBC but may cause
problems when calling the FreeTDS libraries from other languages.  There
are a variety of approaches that could be taken to work around this but for
now you'll have to roll your own.
